\viewkind4\uc1\pard\f0\fs24 (FDOT Financial Project No. 456525-2-58/68-01) Agreement approved and Resolution No. 26-15 adopted, allowing for permanent construction repairs along the Dunedin Causeway, which sustained damage during Hurricane Helene, and providing grant funding in the amount of $1,317,790.00 (County match, $105,424.00; total project cost, $1,423,214.00). Construction to be completed by December 2026. Agreement term ends April 27, 2027.\par
\par
Responding to queries by Chair Eggers, Public Works Director Kelli Hammer Levy indicated that the south side of the Dunedin Causeway sustained approximately $1.4 million in damage from Hurricane Helene; and that the proposed agreement to complete repairs required approval from the Federal Highway Administration and the Florida Department of Transportation; whereupon, she provided details regarding the proposed scope of work.\cf1\f1\fs22\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\f0\fs24 (PID Nos. 003409A and 003747A) Agreements and disclosures approved, as outlined in the Staff Report. Savings amount to approximately $48,000.00 annually, per transformer, totaling an estimated $144,000.00 per year for a five-year period. Agreements expire April 30, 2031.\par
\par
In response to queries by Commissioner Scott, Utilities Financial Services Division Director Linda Benoit indicated that additional backup power and electrical connections were recently installed at the William E. Dunn Water Reclamation Facility; that the rate for interruptible power service at an industrial facility is approximately 40% less expensive than the rate for regular power; and that interruptible service can be accommodated by the availability of on-demand generator backup power.\par
}

